Notebook[{
Cell["SetDirectory[\"DimensionalAnalysis\"];", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
<<DimensionalAnalysisDemo`
<<Miscellaneous`Units`\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
x:=12  Meter
y:=3 Hour
x+y\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3\ Hour + 12\ Meter\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
To prevent operations like the one above,  there is a need for a \
package which would automatically check expressions for their dimensional \
homogenuity. Such a package must also include functions which allow user to \
perform dimensional analysis of equations .  Below there is a demonstration \
on how to perform dimensional analysis (based on Pi theorem) of an unknown \
function provided all parameters are listed. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example from hydrodynamics", "Section"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider the drop of pressure ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " occurring as a result of friction when a  homogenous fluid of constant \
density \[Rho] flows under steady conditions along a  straight pipe of \
constant, circular, cross-sectional area ",
  StyleBox["d. ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " The independent relevant quantities and thier dimensions  are listed \
below:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Here we are not going into details to discuss what  parameters (and \
why)  are  considered to be important for a particular problem. Interested \
reader is referred to Massey (1986). Let us first define some conventional \
notations used in this problem:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData["Parameters:={p, u, \[Mu], \[Rho], l, d};"], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"dimension[p]:=dimension[pressure]; \ndimension[u]:=dimension[velocity]; \n\
dimension[\[Mu]] := dimension[viscosity]; \n\
dimension[\[Rho]]:=dimension[density]; \ndimension[l]:=dimension[length]; \n\
dimension[d]:=dimension[length];\n"], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["dimensions:=dimension[#]&/@Parameters;dimensions", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({M\/\(L\ T\^2\), L\/T, M\/\(L\ T\), M\/L\^3, L, L}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
 The quantity of our primary interest is pressure, so we do not \
want it to appear in the recurring set:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["RestrictedSet:={p, d};", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"According to \[CapitalPi] theorem, we start forming a recurring set  by  \
finding parameters which involve each of the reference magnitudes from \
CurrentBasicUnits:"], "Text"],

Cell["CommonBasicUnits:=MechanicalBasicUnits; ", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
For this problem there are many ways of formulating recurring sets:\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
AllRecSets:=Evaluate[FindRecurringSet[Parameters]]; AllRecSets\
\>",
   "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{\[Mu], l, u}, {\[Rho], l, u}, {\[Rho], \[Mu], u}, {\[Mu], \[Rho], 
        u}, {\[Rho], l, \[Mu]}, {\[Rho], u, \[Mu]}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can, however, indicate that we want, say, ",
  StyleBox["l ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "to be in the recurring set. This is done using an options \
ParInRecSet->{l}"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Options[FindRecurringSet]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({ParInRecSet \[Rule] {}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?ParInRecSet\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("Option in FindRecurringSet to set up any parameters that must be in \
the .reccurring set"\)], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(AllRecSets := 
      Evaluate[FindRecurringSet[Parameters, \ ParInRecSet -> {l}]]; \ 
    AllRecSets\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{\[Mu], l, u}, {\[Rho], l, u}, {\[Rho], l, \[Mu]}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Function NondimensionalRules[] returns rules to nondimensionalize \
all parameters for each recurring set: \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
AllNonDimRules:=Evaluate[NondimensionalRules[AllRecSets, \
Parameters]]; 
AllNonDimRules\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{d \[Rule] d\/l, p \[Rule] \(l\ p\)\/\(u\ \[Mu]\), 
        \[Rho] \[Rule] \(l\ u\ \[Rho]\)\/\[Mu]}, {d \[Rule] d\/l, 
        p \[Rule] p\/\(u\^2\ \[Rho]\), 
        \[Mu] \[Rule] \[Mu]\/\(l\ u\ \[Rho]\)}, {d \[Rule] d\/l, 
        p \[Rule] \(l\^2\ p\ \[Rho]\)\/\[Mu]\^2, 
        u \[Rule] \(l\ u\ \[Rho]\)\/\[Mu]}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Nondimensionalization rules are now substituted into the (unknown) \
function for ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " which now only depends on 5-3=2 parameters (Partonondim): "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Partonondim:=Complement[Parameters, #]&/@AllRecSets; \
Partonondim\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{d, p, \[Rho]}, {d, p, \[Mu]}, {d, p, u}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
newrules:=AllNonDimRules[[1]];
Eqn1:=(p==psample[Sequence@@Complement[Partonondim[[1]], {p}]]) /. newrules; \
Eqn1\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(l\ p\)\/\(u\ \[Mu]\) == psample[d\/l, \(l\ u\ \[Rho]\)\/\[Mu]]\)], 
  "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The next step is usually to find out whether any known named \
dimensionless parameters are encountered in the expression. The first step is \
to go from notations to the descriptions of physical quantities using \
$ToQuantity rules. The default values of these rule are\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \($ToQuantity\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"Dispatch", "[", 
        RowBox[{
        \({p \[Rule] pressure, l \[Rule] length, d \[Rule] length, 
            \[Mu] \[Rule] viscosity, u \[Rule] velocity, 
            \[Rho] \[Rule] density}\), ",", 
          TagBox[
            InterpretationBox[\(-DispatchTables - \),
              1],
            DisplayForm]}], "]"}],
      Function[ BoxForm`arg, 
        ReplacePart[ 
          HoldComplete[ 
            Dispatch[ ProgrammingDump`dumy]], 
          HoldComplete[ BoxForm`arg], {1, 1}, {1, 1}], {HoldAllComplete}]]], 
  "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
can easily be changed by a user.  Then, a set of rules for changing \
certain combinations of physical quantities into Named dimensionless \
paramters ($ToNamedDimensionlessPara) is  applied. Here \
NamedDimensionlessPara includes only Reynolds number, Re.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(arglist := Complement[Partonondim[\([1]\)], \ {p}]\  /. \ newrules; 
    arglist\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({d\/l, \(l\ u\ \[Rho]\)\/\[Mu]}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(argrules := Evaluate[RulesToDimensionlessPara[arglist]]; \ argrules\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({\(l\ u\ \[Rho]\)\/\[Mu] \[Rule] Re}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newarglist := arglist\  /. \ argrules; \ newarglist\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({d\/l, Re}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(eqn1 := \((p\  /. \ newrules)\) == psample[Sequence@@newarglist]; \ eqn1
    \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(l\ p\)\/\(u\ \[Mu]\) == psample[d\/l, Re]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Similarly, for two other recurring sets:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
newrules:=AllNonDimRules[[2]]; 
arglist:=Complement[Partonondim[[2]], {p}] /. newrules; 
argrules:=Evaluate[RulesToDimensionlessPara[arglist]]; 
newarglist:=arglist /. argrules; 
Eqn2:=(p /. newrules) ==psample[Sequence@@newarglist]; Eqn2
\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(p\/\(u\^2\ \[Rho]\) == psample[d\/l, 1\/Re]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
newrules:=AllNonDimRules[[3]]; 
arglist:=Complement[Partonondim[[3]], {p}] /. newrules; 
argrules:=Evaluate[RulesToDimensionlessPara[arglist]]; 
newarglist:=arglist /. argrules; 
Eqn3:=(p /. newrules)==psample[Sequence@@newarglist]; Eqn3
\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(l\^2\ p\ \[Rho]\)\/\[Mu]\^2 == psample[d\/l, Re]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[
"All three recurring sets and their respective solutions are equally good \
depending on what relationships are under study. For example, if the effect   \
of viscosity is to be studied, Eqn. 1 or 3 are rather inconvinient, as \[Mu]  \
 appears in both Re and in nondimensional pressure. For this purpose, Eqn 2   \
where \[Mu] only appears in Re would be better. "], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["On cooking and dimensional analysis", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This example is taken from the paper \"On cooking a roast\" by \
M.S.Klamkin (SIAM Review, 1961, Vol.3(2), 167-169).
The main aim of the Klamkin's note is to establish how the \"proper\" time of \
cooking depends on the weight of a roast. 
General assumptions are first that the roasts form a homogeneous isotropic \
homothetic family; secondly, that the oven is maintained at a fixed roasting \
temperature; thirdly, that the roasts have the same initial temperature; and \
that the roasts are considered to be properly cooked when the center \
temperature reaches a certain fixed temperature. So, parameters defining the \
problem are: \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Parameters := {l, \ c, \ \[Rho], \ t, \ \[Gamma]}; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"Here c is heat capacity, \[Gamma] is thermal conductivity, \[Rho] is \
density, l is characteristic length (e.g. diameter of the piece of roast),  \
and t is time to cook the roast till fixed temperature \[Theta]o. "], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[
"dimension[c]:=dimension[heatcapacity]; \n\
dimension[\[Gamma]]:=dimension[thermalconductivity]; \n\
dimension[\[Rho]]:=dimension[density]; \ndimension[l]:=dimension[length]; \n\
dimension[t]:=dimension[time];\n"], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["dimensions:=dimension[#]&/@Parameters;dimensions", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({L, L\^2\/\(K\ T\^2\), M\/L\^3, T, \(L\ M\)\/\(K\ T\^3\)}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[
" The quantity of our primary interest is time and we want to know how it \
depends on the size (or weight) of the roast. As the roas is considered to be \
properly cooked at certain fixed temperature, \[Theta] is also excluded from \
the recurring set. "], "Text"],

Cell["RestrictedSet:={t};", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"According to \[CapitalPi] theorem, we start forming a recurring set  by  \
finding parameters which involve each of the reference magnitudes from \
CurrentBasicUnits:"], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
CommonBasicUnits:=ThermodynamicalBasicUnits; CommonBasicUnits\
\>", 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({M, L, T, K}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
For this problem there are many ways of formulating recurring sets:\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
AllRecSets:=Evaluate[FindRecurringSet[Parameters]]; AllRecSets\
\>",
   "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{c, l, \[Gamma], \[Rho]}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
AllNonDimRules:=Evaluate[NondimensionalRules[AllRecSets, \
Parameters]]; 
AllNonDimRules\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{t \[Rule] \(t\ \[Gamma]\)\/\(c\ l\^2\ \[Rho]\)}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Substitution of these nondimensionalization rules into the \
(unknown) dependency of time on other roast parameters yields\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Partonondim:=Complement[Parameters, #]&/@AllRecSets; \
Partonondim\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{t}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
newrules:=AllNonDimRules[[1]];
Eqn1:=(t==tsample[Sequence@@Complement[Partonondim[[1]], {t}]]) /. newrules; \
Eqn1\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(t\ \[Gamma]\)\/\(c\ l\^2\ \[Rho]\) == tsample[]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
As all roasts are assumed to have similar heat capacity, thermal \
conductivity and density, we obtain from the previous that time to cook a \
roast is simply proportional to the square of some characteristic length of \
the roast:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(t~l^2*\[Phi]sample[]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"Here \[Phi]sample[] is a constant  (of dimension T*L^(-2)).   Weight of the \
roast is proportional to the cube of the length w~l^3, thus resulting in a \
very important conclusion. The proper time of cooking the proper roast is \
proportional to the two-thirds power of its weight! This result has been \
obtained by Murray Klamkin (1961) who argured with the cookbooks suggesting \
that the time of cooking is simply proportional to its weight.   Excellent \
example demonstrating dimensional analysis for cooks. SAY ABOUT TURKEY AS \
WELL!!!"], "Text"]
}, Open  ]]
},
FrontEndVersion->"X 3.0",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1280}, {0, 1024}},
WindowSize->{765, 913},
WindowMargins->{{Automatic, 136}, {5, Automatic}}
]


